==English==
[[File:Keychain with text "Encryption is key".jpg|right|thumb|A [[keyring]] is a helix containing two '''convolutions''' (360° turns).]]
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
Borrowed from {{bor|en|la|convolutus||to roll together}}, past participle of {{m|la|convolvere}}, from {{m|la|con-}} + {{m|la|volvere||to roll}}, with the suffix {{m|en|-tion}}. Equivalent to {{suffix|en|convolute|ion}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{rhymes|en|uːʃən|s=4}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# A [[twist]] or [[fold]].
# Any of the [[fold]]s on the [[surface]] of the [[brain]].
# The shape of something [[rotating]]; a [[vortex]].
# The state or condition of being [[convoluted]].
# {{lb|en|mathematics|functional analysis}} A mathematical operation on two functions that produces a third that expresses how the shape of one is modified by the other; the [[integral]] of the product of the two functions after one is reflected about the ''y''-axis and shifted along the ''x''-axis.
#: {{coordinate terms|en|deconvolution}}
#* '''1997''', Richard Tolimieri, Myoung An, Chao Lu, ''Algorithms for Discrete Fourier Transform and '''Convolution''''', 2nd Edition, Springer, [https://books.google.com.au/books?id=Na3hBwAAQBAJ&pg=PA101&dq=%22convolution%22%7C%22convolutions%22&hl=en&newbks=1&newbks_redir=0&sa=X&ved=2ahUKEwjpmf_sopn7AhU47TgGHSilCCsQ6AF6BAheEAI#v=onepage&q=%22convolution%22%7C%22convolutions%22&f=false page 101],
#*: Linear '''convolution''' is one of the most frequent computations carried out in digital signal processing (DSP).
#* '''1994''', Semen B. Yakubovich, Yurii F. Luchko, ''The Hypergeometric Approach to Integral Transforms and '''Convolutions''''', Springer, [https://books.google.com.au/books?id=VKz9CAAAQBAJ&pg=PA183&dq=%22convolution%22%7C%22convolutions%22&hl=en&newbks=1&newbks_redir=0&sa=X&ved=2ahUKEwjpmf_sopn7AhU47TgGHSilCCsQ6AF6BAhhEAI#v=onepage&q=%22convolution%22%7C%22convolutions%22&f=false page 183],
#*: In Chapter 11 we considered <math>H</math>-'''convolutions''' of generalized <math>H</math>-transforms. These '''convolutions''' are bilinear, commutative and associative operations{{...}}.
# {{lb|en|computing}} A function which maps a [[tuple]] of [[sequences]] into a sequence of tuples.
# One 360° turn in a spring or similar helix.

====Usage notes====
* {{sense|functional analysis}} The term refers to both the result function and to the process of computing it.

